<h2>Where the ground came from</h2>
<p>Before 2015, "patent illegality" lived inside the public-policy ground, judicially developed and uncomfortably elastic. The Arbitration and Conciliation (Amendment) Act, 2015 gave it a statutory home — Section 34(2A) — and simultaneously fenced it: the ground applies only to domestic awards, the illegality must appear on the face of the award, and the provision\'s proviso forbids setting aside for mere error of law or on re-appreciation of evidence. The design is deliberate. Parliament preserved a safety valve for awards that are lawless in a visible way, while shutting the door on the disappointed party\'s instinct to retry the case under the label of illegality.</p>

<h2>The working test</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old">
<h3>Not patent illegality</h3>
<p>A wrong-but-arguable reading of a contract clause. A preference for one witness over another. A damages computation the court finds generous. An error of law that required argument to expose. Each of these is, at worst, an erroneous application of law or a different view of evidence — and the statute protects the award against all of them.</p>
</div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new">
<h3>Patent illegality</h3>
<p>Answering a question the claim never asked. Importing a precondition the contract expressly excludes. Ignoring the governing statute. Internally contradictory findings that decide nothing. The defect must be visible on the award\'s own face and must go to the root of the matter — remove it, and the operative result cannot stand.</p>
</div>
</div>

<h2>The doctrine at work: a claim-by-claim illustration</h2>
<p>The Delhi High Court\'s Division Bench decision in <em>MMTC Ltd v. Chauhan Jewellers</em> (27 October 2026) shows the discipline applied with unusual clarity, because the same award yielded both outcomes. Of five export-consignment claims a tribunal had rejected, three rejections were upheld as possible views of conflicting evidence — the court refusing to reappraise who caused a redirection of parcels or a short realisation. Two rejections fell: one because the tribunal rejected an interest-on-delay claim by inquiring into a shortfall that was never the question, misreading cash-on-delivery terms as letter-of-credit terms; the other because the tribunal made collection of returned goods a precondition to a financing claim that the contract\'s direct-recovery provisions expressly did not require. The consequential direction — return of mortgaged title deeds — fell with the findings that supported it. One award, five claims, two illegalities: the ground worked exactly as designed.</p>

<h2>Drafting the challenge — and resisting it</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
<li>Plead by finding, not by grievance: identify the paragraph of the award, the text it contradicts, and why the result cannot survive the defect.</li>
<li>Stay on the face of the award. The moment the argument needs the transcript or the exhibits re-weighed, it has left Section 34(2A).</li>
<li>Argue severability both ways: challengers should isolate infected findings; award-holders should show the operative result stands independently of the attacked reasoning.</li>
<li>Remember the forum\'s limits persist on appeal: Section 37 adds no wider jurisdiction, so an argument that fails the 34(2A) test below fails it above.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>The deeper lesson is for the arbitration itself. Awards attract patent-illegality challenges when their reasoning drifts from the questions the pleadings posed. Tribunals that frame each claim precisely, answer the claim as framed, and tie every precondition to the contract\'s text leave Section 34(2A) nothing to bite on — and parties who want durable awards should press their tribunals, claim by claim, to do exactly that.</p>
</div>
